#!/bin/sh
# The gate on this repository, which is `headwater check` and one clause that
# reads no engine at all: a commit that removes a file from `site/`, the
# directory Cloudflare serves, is refused. That clause states its own reasons
# where it stands, below.
#
# Install once per clone (the repo ships the hook, git does not install it):
#     git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# What blocks is what the engine reports as an error, which is the fixability
# bar of spec 12 rather than a judgment about how much a defect matters. A
# contraction, a British spelling, a hard-wrapped block and a retired term that
# names its replacement are all repaired mechanically, so they block. A sentence
# past the word limit, a semicolon in running prose and a stock metaphor are
# repaired by rewriting, so they stay advisory and reach you in the CI summary.
#
# Two differences from the script this replaced. It read the staged content and
# this reads the working tree, so a partial stage is checked as the tree stands.
# And it needed only a Python interpreter, where this needs a built engine: the
# hook fails open when there is none, and says so in one line, because the CI
# job builds the engine and runs the same check on every pull request.
#
# # The change this commit carries
#
# Two rules of the engine declare `needs_prior`, and an instance of one reaches
# no verdict in a run that carries no change. `warrant.promoted` counts the
# promotions, and `lifecycle.transition.not_permitted` refuses a state movement
# the declared machine does not admit. That second one is an error severity, so
# this is where it blocks: an illegal movement is only visible against the
# version that stood before it, and a working-tree hook is the one gate that has
# it.
#
# `change-manifest` beside this file is what turns `HEAD` and the working tree
# into the manifest the engine reads. Spec 12 fixes `HEAD` as the anchor here
# and the merge base as the anchor for a proposed change, which is what the CI
# job passes.
#
# It fails open, like the line above it: no git, no `mktemp`, or a `HEAD` this
# clone does not hold, and the commit is checked without a change rather than
# refused. The cost of failing open is one rule that reports a skip, and the
# cost of failing closed is a repository nobody can commit to.

root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel) || exit 0

# `site/` is what Cloudflare serves as https://headwater.tools/, with no build
# step, on every push to the default branch. `mkdocs build` cleans its
# `site_dir` before it writes, so a configuration whose `site_dir` resolves
# onto `site/` empties it and exits 0 with no warning. #430 is that hazard. The
# CI job catches the configuration; this catches the bytes, which is the half
# that is still ahead of the deploy on a push to `main`.
#
# It reads the index rather than the working tree, unlike every other reading
# this hook takes, because what reaches Cloudflare is what the commit carries.
#
# Above the engine check on purpose: that check exits 0 on a clone with no
# built engine, and this clause needs no engine.
#
# The variable releases this clause and nothing below it, and on this tree that
# matters for three files. HW-DR-0037 declares `governs` over the eight pages it
# names, HW-DR-0047 declares it over `site/_headers`, and both oblige a page
# added under `site/` to carry an edge of its own. So removing any of those nine
# is refused a second time by `relation.target.unresolved`, which names the
# record that has to move first.
#
# `site/llms.txt`, `site/robots.txt` and `site/sitemap.xml` are the three the
# variable releases on its own. No record names them,
# `tools/refresh-crawler-files.sh` writes all three from the pages under
# `site/`, and a crawler reads each one at a fixed path with no link to follow.
# So this clause is the whole gate on exactly the files whose loss is silent.
if [ -z "${HEADWATER_ALLOW_SITE_DELETE:-}" ]; then
    gone=$(git -C "$root" diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=D -- site/)
    if [ -n "$gone" ]; then
        echo "headwater: this commit deletes a file from \`site/\`, which Cloudflare serves"
        echo "  as https://headwater.tools/ with no build step. \`mkdocs build\` cleans its"
        echo "  \`site_dir\` before it writes, so a configuration whose \`site_dir\` resolves"
        echo "  onto \`site/\` empties it and exits 0. Deleted here:"
        printf '%s\n' "$gone" | sed 's/^/    /'
        echo "  If the removal is deliberate:"
        echo "        HEADWATER_ALLOW_SITE_DELETE=1 git commit ..."
        exit 1
    fi
fi

# The merge driver for a derived artifact needs one command per clone, because
# git will not take a driver from a repository: it is an executable, and a clone
# that ran one it had not been asked about would be running code it never read.
# So the attribute in `.gitattributes` selects the file and this config supplies
# the driver, and a clone with the first and not the second falls back to an
# ordinary merge. That failure is silent by construction — the merge succeeds,
# and what it leaves is a count true of neither branch — so this reports it.
#
# It reports and does not block. A clone that has not run the command is behind
# on a setup step, which is not a defect in the commit in front of it, and the
# same reasoning is why the missing engine above exits 0.
if [ -z "$(git config merge.headwater-regenerate.driver 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
    echo "headwater: this clone has no merge driver for a derived artifact, so a"
    echo "  merge of one would be reconciled rather than refused. Set it once:"
    echo "        git config merge.headwater-regenerate.name \"regenerate a derived artifact\""
    echo "        git config merge.headwater-regenerate.driver \".githooks/merge-regenerate %O %A %B %P\""
fi

# `core.hooksPath` is shared by every worktree of a clone, and an absolute value
# names one checkout. A worktree on another branch then runs that checkout's
# hook body while committing its own, which reads exactly like a gate that
# passed: a clause the branch carries does not run, and a clause it does not
# carry can refuse it naming a file the branch never had. A relative value
# resolves inside the working tree the commit happens in, which is the one
# `CLAUDE.md` instructs.
#
# It reports and does not block, for the reason the driver above gives and for
# one more. Nobody sets this value by hand: the `EnterWorktree` tool of the
# harness rewrites it to an absolute path on every call and never restores it,
# so a refusal would punish the committer for a tool the committer did not
# choose to run. The last line says the value comes back, because a reader who
# fixes it, watches it return and was not told stops believing the report.
#
# One consequence works in this clause's favor. Under an absolute value every
# worktree runs the main checkout's copy of this file, so the report reaches a
# worktree as soon as the main checkout carries it, whatever branch the
# worktree is on.
hooks_path=$(git config --get core.hooksPath 2>/dev/null)
case $hooks_path in
    /*)
        echo "headwater: core.hooksPath is absolute ($hooks_path), so every worktree of"
        echo "  this clone runs the hook body of that one checkout instead of its own"
        echo "  branch's. Set it relative, once per clone:"
        echo "        git config core.hooksPath .githooks"
        echo "  \`EnterWorktree\` sets it absolute again on every call, so expect this"
        echo "  line back after the next one, and set it again."
        ;;
esac

# Either profile builds an engine this gate can run, and the newer of the two
# answers. `release` is what CI builds and what a release artifact ships;
# `dev-release` is the same optimization level without the `lto = true` and
# `codegen-units = 1` link, which is single threaded and costs minutes. This
# gate read the `release` path alone, so a clone that wanted a checked commit
# bought that link for a binary this hook runs for a fifth of a second.
#
# The newer one wins rather than `release` by name: a stale `release` binary
# beside a `dev-release` one built after it would check the commit through an
# engine older than the change in front of it, which is a quieter failure than
# the cost. `.claude/hooks/lib.sh` resolves the same two paths the same way, and
# `sh .githooks/fixtures.sh` and `sh .claude/hooks/fixtures.sh` hold each copy.
release="$root/engine/target/release/headwater"
dev_release="$root/engine/target/dev-release/headwater"
engine=
[ -x "$release" ] && engine=$release
if [ -x "$dev_release" ] && { [ -z "$engine" ] || [ "$dev_release" -nt "$engine" ]; }; then
    engine=$dev_release
fi
if [ -z "$engine" ]; then
    echo "headwater: no built engine, so this commit is not checked for prose."
    echo "  build it once:"
    echo "        cargo build --profile dev-release -p headwater-cli --manifest-path engine/Cargo.toml --locked"
    echo "  that is the cheap profile, and this gate runs it. Build --release when"
    echo "  what you want is the shipped artifact rather than a checked commit:"
    echo "        cargo build --release -p headwater-cli --manifest-path engine/Cargo.toml --locked"
    exit 0
fi

manifest=
work=$(mktemp -d 2>/dev/null)
if [ -n "$work" ]; then
    trap 'rm -rf "$work"' EXIT HUP INT TERM
    manifest=$(sh "$root/.githooks/change-manifest" HEAD "$work" 2>"$work/why")
fi
if [ -z "$manifest" ]; then
    echo "headwater: this commit carries no described change, so a state movement is"
    echo "  checked by nothing. Every other rule still runs."
    [ -n "$work" ] && [ -s "$work/why" ] && sed 's/^/  /' "$work/why"
fi

if [ -n "$manifest" ]; then
    report=$("$engine" check --strict --change "$manifest" 2>&1)
else
    report=$("$engine" check --strict 2>&1)
fi
status=$?

# `site/llms.txt`, `site/robots.txt` and `site/sitemap.xml` are derived from the
# `<title>`, the `<meta name="description">` and the path of every page
# committed under `site/`, so a page added, removed or retitled makes them stale
# and nothing else notices.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT THAT PUT THIS HERE. Since the two files were introduced on
# 2026-08-27, three commits moved a title, a description or the set of pages.
# Two updated the files. One did not — `0219db3`, fourteen minutes after the
# mechanism landed — and the repair cost a second commit, `19b8d3e`, and an
# issue of its own, #443. One in three, and a miss is not free.
#
# WHY IT REFUSES RATHER THAN REGENERATES. A hook that writes produces a commit
# whose contents nobody read, and it would stage bytes the author never saw
# into a commit the clauses above already checked against different ones. The
# writing form is the script, run by hand — the separation `headwater check
# --fix` already keeps.
#
# WHY IT SITS HERE AND NOT ABOVE. Deleting a page under `site/` necessarily
# makes both files stale, which is the same act the `HEADWATER_ALLOW_SITE_DELETE`
# clause governs and the same act that can leave a `governs` edge dangling.
# Placed earlier, this clause fired first and reported a derived file while
# hiding a `relation.target.unresolved` finding underneath it. An engine finding
# outranks a regenerable one, so this runs only once the engine has passed.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CATCH. A clone with no built engine exits above this line, so
# the check does not run there. The script also reads the working tree and not
# the index, so a partially staged commit can pass here. The CI step of the same
# name reads the committed tree, and that is what actually holds this.
#
# COST. 0.13 seconds, against about 1.0 for the rest of this hook.
if [ "$status" -eq 0 ] && [ -z "${HEADWATER_SKIP_CRAWLER_CHECK:-}" ]; then
    if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        if ! crawler=$(sh "$root/tools/refresh-crawler-files.sh" --check 2>&1); then
            echo "headwater: a derived file under \`site/\` no longer matches the pages"
            echo "  committed there. \`llms.txt\`, \`robots.txt\` and \`sitemap.xml\` are all"
            echo "  written by \`tools/refresh-crawler-files.sh\` and none of them by hand."
            printf '%s\n' "$crawler" | sed 's/^/    /'
            echo "  Regenerate and stage the result:"
            echo "        sh tools/refresh-crawler-files.sh"
            echo "  Or skip this one clause for one commit:"
            echo "        HEADWATER_SKIP_CRAWLER_CHECK=1 git commit ..."
            exit 1
        fi
    else
        echo "headwater: no python3, so the crawler files are not checked."
    fi
fi

# The shared visual register of the hand-built pages, which HW-DR-0050 rules.
# `tools/site-tokens.css` is the one copy of it, and every page carries the
# block between its two `headwater:tokens` markers. A page cannot link that
# file: `site/_headers` gives every hand-built path `default-src 'none'` with
# no `'self'`, so the copy happens before the commit and the committed bytes
# are what Cloudflare serves.
#
# WHY IT SITS HERE. The same reason as the clause above. A page deleted or
# added under `site/` moves this too, and an engine finding outranks a
# regenerable one, so this runs only once the engine has passed.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CATCH. The script reads the working tree rather than the
# index, so a partially staged commit can pass here. The CI step of the same
# name reads the committed tree, and that is what holds it.
if [ "$status" -eq 0 ] && [ -z "${HEADWATER_SKIP_TOKEN_CHECK:-}" ]; then
    if command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        if ! tokens=$(sh "$root/tools/refresh-site-tokens.sh" --check 2>&1); then
            echo "headwater: a hand-built page under \`site/\` carries a visual register"
            echo "  that disagrees with \`tools/site-tokens.css\`, which is the one copy"
            echo "  of it. The block between the \`headwater:tokens\` markers is written"
            echo "  by \`tools/refresh-site-tokens.sh\` and never by hand."
            printf '%s\n' "$tokens" | sed 's/^/    /'
            echo "  Edit \`tools/site-tokens.css\`, regenerate, and stage the result:"
            echo "        sh tools/refresh-site-tokens.sh"
            echo "  Or skip this one clause for one commit:"
            echo "        HEADWATER_SKIP_TOKEN_CHECK=1 git commit ..."
            exit 1
        fi
    else
        echo "headwater: no python3, so the site tokens are not checked."
    fi
fi

# The figures on the hand-built pages, which HW-DR-0039 rules. Every number a
# page under `site/` states comes from a run of this engine, written into a
# `data-figure` element by `tools/refresh-figures.sh`, and the page carries no
# source of its own. HW-DR-0039 recorded that nothing ran the check. This is
# the first of the two callers that now do.
#
# WHY IT SITS HERE. The same reason as the two clauses above. A page added or
# deleted under `site/` moves this too, and an engine finding outranks a
# regenerable one, so this runs only once the engine has passed. It is also
# below the built-engine guard, which is the only correct place for it: the
# script runs the engine three times and exits 2 without a binary.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT CATCH. A clone with no built engine exits above this line.
# The script reads the working tree rather than the index, so a partially
# staged commit can pass here. And the date of a run is not compared, because
# it is the clock rather than a function of the tree — the script's own header
# states that. The CI step named "The figures on the hand-built pages came
# from a run" reads the committed tree, has no escape hatch and no skip
# variable, and is the half that holds.
#
# WHY THE `python3` GUARD, WHICH LETS A COMMIT THROUGH. `refresh-figures.sh`
# runs `python3` under `set -eu`, so a missing interpreter makes it exit
# non-zero and this clause would refuse the commit while reporting stale
# figures, which is a refusal whose message is false. The guard turns a lying
# refusal into a printed line. `mktemp` is not covered the same way: the script
# calls it itself, so a host with no `mktemp` fails closed here, and the
# script's own error is printed in the indented block below.
#
# COST, AND WHY IT IS WORTH IT. This clause runs the engine three times, so it
# is the most expensive one in this file by a wide margin. No number is written
# here on purpose: two measurements of it on one machine on one day, under
# different loads, disagreed by a factor of six, and a timing with no load
# beside it misleads. Measure it where you are, with
# `time sh tools/refresh-figures.sh --check`.
#
# What justifies the cost is the miss rate rather than the seconds. The crawler
# clause above was adopted on a measured one-in-three miss over the life of the
# files it guards. This one is measured at a hundred percent over three weeks:
# nothing ran this check between 2026-08-26 and 2026-09-06, every corpus-moving
# commit in that window staled a figure, and 59 figure occurrences and 11
# verbatim tutorial blocks were adrift when it was first run again.
if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then
    if [ -n "${HEADWATER_SKIP_FIGURE_CHECK:-}" ]; then
        # A skip nobody can see is a skip nobody counts. The hook cannot write
        # into the commit, so this line on the author's terminal is the whole
        # local record, and the CI step above is the durable half.
        echo "headwater: HEADWATER_SKIP_FIGURE_CHECK is set, so the figures on the"
        echo "  hand-built pages are not checked. CI checks them with no such hatch."
    elif command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        if ! figures=$(sh "$root/tools/refresh-figures.sh" --check 2>&1); then
            echo "headwater: a figure on a hand-built page under \`site/\` disagrees with"
            echo "  a fresh run of this engine. Every such figure is written by"
            echo "  \`tools/refresh-figures.sh\` from a run, and none of them by hand."
            printf '%s\n' "$figures" | sed 's/^/    /'
            echo "  Measure again and stage the result:"
            echo "        sh tools/refresh-figures.sh"
            echo "  A verbatim tutorial block is the one thing that command will not"
            echo "  repair. Copy the block out of the document it names."
            echo "  Or skip this one clause for one commit:"
            echo "        HEADWATER_SKIP_FIGURE_CHECK=1 git commit ..."
            exit 1
        fi
    else
        echo "headwater: no python3, so the figures on the hand-built pages are not"
        echo "  checked."
    fi
fi

[ "$status" -eq 0 ] && exit 0

# The error findings alone, whole. Every other finding is advisory and belongs
# in the report a reader asks for rather than in a hook that just refused a
# commit.
#
# A finding is a location line that ends in `error` and every line indented
# further than it — the rule line, the `fix:` line, and the continuations of
# both. #340 lays the report out at 80 columns, so a message is two or three
# lines now where it used to be one, and the rule this replaced printed exactly
# one line after the header. That rule truncated a refused commit's explanation
# mid-sentence, which is the one moment the explanation is read.
#
# The indent decides where the finding stops, so nothing here needs to know how
# many lines a message took. The continuation clause is tested before the header
# clause, so a folded line whose last word happens to be `error` is read as the
# continuation it is rather than as a second finding.
#
# `NF > 1` is the second guard, and it is about a shape rather than a width. A
# header is `<path>:<line>:<column> error`, which is two fields. A line whose
# whole content is the word `error` is one field and is never a header, whatever
# the indent says. The fill will not emit one — `headwater_check::fill` leaves a
# location line whole rather than wrapping its severity away — and this line is
# what keeps a future change to the fill from silently emptying this hook.
printf '%s\n' "$report" | awk '
    { at = match($0, /[^ ]/); indent = at - 1 }
    show && at > 0 && indent > base { print; next }
    / error$/ && NF > 1 { print; show = 1; base = indent; next }
    { show = 0 }
'
cat <<'EOF'

`headwater check --strict` reports an error over this corpus. Your options:
  - read them all: headwater check
  - fix them, which for every error-severity rule is a mechanical edit
  - mark a deliberate exception on the offending block:
        <!-- headwater allow=<rule> scope=block until=<YYYY-MM-DD> reason=false_positive note=<why> -->
  - declare it as debt with an owner and an expiry: headwater infer --owner <name> --write
  - skip this check for one commit: git commit --no-verify
EOF
exit 1
